From 96a0d13b6e98ad4ef86e66a3f6ddb5dd9199a591 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: halt95 <halt95@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 02:49:52 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 97/97] kv cache: hold a finished turn's prefix until its next
 turn is admitted

With a prefix unit finer than the block, a hybrid follow-up resumes from
a single GDN state at the previous prompt's tail, and that key sits in
the top attention block. Losing any one block of that prefix is a total
miss. The waiting-request pins only protect a hit that is still whole
when the follow-up is first looked up. Tracing the remaining misses
under a long-context multi-turn agent load shows they lost their top
attention block before that, in the gap between the previous turn
finishing and the next turn arriving. In that gap nothing referenced
the prefix. LRU then consumed it ahead of dead blocks freed later
(other conversations' handed-over CoW sources and superseded states).

A finished turn (stop, length, repetition, or an abort after decoding)
now keeps one extra ref on each of its hashed blocks, including its GDN
holds, as a grace pin. The ref is taken on both free paths, the
immediate free and the deferred pop_blocks_for_free behind an in-flight
step, so the prefix never passes through the free queue. A grace is
released when:

- the next turn is admitted on it. That is the grace holding the
  deepest hit block of every group, with a hit that reaches its prompt
  to within two prefix units, so a new turn sharing only a system
  prompt releases nothing. The table takes the blocks over, with no
  double ref.
- anyone needs blocks. Graces yield before waiter pins, oldest first and
  one per failed allocation, so they never cause a preemption.
- the bound (16) is exceeded. The oldest grace goes.
- reset_prefix_cache runs.

A head waiter whose admission fails pins its own hit first. It then
yields graces and other pins one at a time, and only when everything
yieldable covers its shortfall. Otherwise yielding would drop other
conversations' prefixes and still not admit it. The shortfall is
measured after the pin: the pin takes the proof block past the hit (and
any hit block the lookup did not return) out of the free queue, so the
shortfall of the failed attempt can be short by those blocks and the
guard would yield another conversation's grace for nothing. Its own
pinned blocks outside the hit it is admitted on, the proof block past
it, go back first: once the hit is taken over they protect nothing, and
they cost nobody else anything. At an exact fit they are the blocks it
needs, and nothing else ever yields a head waiter's own pin, so without
this a request that needs the whole pool waited forever.

While any pin or grace is held the scheduler reports no common prefix
to cascade attention. Those refs can make a block's count equal the
number of running requests when only some of them share it, and cascade
attention would then read it for all of them.

Graces and waiter pins now follow a producer's GDN hash into its CoW
copy, as holds already do. Otherwise they would keep a second ref on a
running state block that its owner must own exclusively. Graces apply
only to fine-grained hybrid hits (coordinator.enable_partial_hash_hits)
and are off with a KV connector; a block-sized unit (the 4096 lane) is
unchanged. When graces are active the scheduler logs one startup line
saying so, with the bound, the hash unit and the block size.

Tests: the arrival-gap reproduction fails on the previous commit (hit 0
instead of 168, both free paths). It passes here. Further tests cover
the handover refcounts, yield order and bound, finish statuses, a
running request at an exactly full pool, yieldable accounting,
moved-hash following, the pin's extras contract, and six runs on the
real scheduler: among them a head waiter whose pin raises its shortfall
(it gives its proof block back and the one grace that covers the rest
yields), a request that needs the whole pool (admitted on its own proof
block; it waited forever before), and the common-prefix count under a
grace (none reported; the count returns once the pins are gone).
